## Testing Cache-Dependent Code

## The Core Problem

Cache introduces state between calls. Without controlling that state, tests are order-dependent and non-deterministic:

```typescript
// ❌ Flaky — test result depends on what ran before it
test('fetches user from API on first call', async () => {
  const user = await getUser('1'); // might be cached from a previous test!
  expect(api.fetchUser).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
});
```

## Step 1: Abstract the Cache Behind an Interface

```typescript
interface Cache {
  get<T>(key: string): T | undefined;
  set<T>(key: string, value: T, ttlMs?: number): void;
  delete(key: string): void;
  clear(): void;
}

// Real implementation (production)
class RedisCache implements Cache { ... }

// In-memory implementation (tests)
class InMemoryCache implements Cache {
  private store = new Map<string, { value: unknown; expiresAt?: number }>();

  get<T>(key: string): T | undefined {
    const entry = this.store.get(key);
    if (!entry) return undefined;
    if (entry.expiresAt && Date.now() > entry.expiresAt) {
      this.store.delete(key);
      return undefined;
    }
    return entry.value as T;
  }

  set<T>(key: string, value: T, ttlMs?: number): void {
    this.store.set(key, {
      value,
      expiresAt: ttlMs ? Date.now() + ttlMs : undefined,
    });
  }

  delete(key: string) { this.store.delete(key); }
  clear() { this.store.clear(); }
}
```

## Step 2: Test Cache-Miss and Cache-Hit Separately

```typescript
let cache: InMemoryCache;
let userService: UserService;
const mockFetch = jest.fn();

beforeEach(() => {
  cache = new InMemoryCache(); // fresh cache per test
  userService = new UserService(mockFetch, cache);
});

// Cache miss — fetches from source
test('calls API on first request', async () => {
  mockFetch.mockResolvedValue({ id: '1', name: 'Aaron' });

  await userService.getUser('1');

  expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
});

// Cache hit — does not call API again
test('returns cached user without calling API again', async () => {
  mockFetch.mockResolvedValue({ id: '1', name: 'Aaron' });

  await userService.getUser('1'); // populates cache
  await userService.getUser('1'); // should use cache

  expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1); // only called once
});
```

## Step 3: Test TTL Expiry with Fake Timers

```typescript
test('re-fetches after TTL expires', async () => {
  jest.useFakeTimers();
  mockFetch.mockResolvedValue({ id: '1', name: 'Aaron' });

  await userService.getUser('1'); // cache populated (TTL: 60s)
  jest.advanceTimersByTime(61_000); // fast-forward past TTL
  await userService.getUser('1'); // should re-fetch

  expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);
  jest.useRealTimers();
});
```

## Step 4: Test Cache Invalidation

```typescript
test('invalidates cache on update', async () => {
  mockFetch.mockResolvedValue({ id: '1', name: 'Aaron' });
  await userService.getUser('1'); // cache hit

  await userService.updateUser('1', { name: 'Aaron Updated' });

  // After update, cache for this user should be cleared
  await userService.getUser('1');
  expect(mockFetch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2); // fetched fresh after update
});
```

## Summary

```
Fresh cache per test     → no state leakage between tests
In-memory fake in units  → fast, deterministic
Fake timers for TTL      → test expiry without real waiting
Real cache in integration → verify Redis/LRU actual behaviour
```
